course: dictionary
lesson: 13863
title: Justification
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A justification is a good reason that shows something is right or fair. «Sam was late because his bus broke down.» The broken bus is a justification: it explains why Sam was late, and it shows his lateness was not his fault.
explain: In maths, a justification explains how you know an answer is correct. «I know 7 times 8 is 56, because 7 times 4 is 28 and double 28 is 56.» That is a justification. Just writing 56 is not.
explain: To give a justification, say why. A good test is to ask: does this reason really show the thing is right? If it does not, it is not a justification.
fact: A justification is a good reason that shows something is right or fair.
fact: «There is no justification for lying» means there is no good reason that makes lying right.
fact: «There is some justification for the rule» means there is a little good reason behind the rule.
fact: In maths, «show your justification» means explain your reasoning, not just give the answer.
fact: In printing and computers, justification means lining up text so both edges of a column are straight.
exercise: «Sam was late because his bus broke down.» What is the justification for Sam being late? | He likes walking slowly | *His bus broke down | He was lazy
exercise: In maths, «show your justification» means what? | Give the answer as quickly as you can | Draw a picture only | Write the answer in big letters | *Explain your reasoning, not just give the answer
exercise: The teacher asks, «Can you give a justification for your answer?» What does she want? | A loud answer | A guess | A drawing of the answer | *A good reason that shows your answer is right
test: Tom says, «There is no justification for breaking the rule.» What does this mean? | Tom likes rules | *There is no good reason that makes breaking the rule right | Rules are very long | Breaking the rule is always fine
test: «The lines of text are justified so both edges are straight.» Which meaning of justified is this? | Rushed in a hurry | Shouted at for doing wrong | *Lined up so both edges are straight, as in printing | Excused from doing a task
exam: «Ella gave a justification for her answer.» What did Ella do? | She guessed the answer | She wrote the answer twice in big letters | She changed the answer without any reason | *She explained why her answer was right
